To connect an EC2 instance to an AWS Load Balancer, you normally use a Target Group.

The flow is:

User → Load Balancer → Target Group → EC2

Steps
Create EC2
EC2 must be running.
Your application should be running on a port, for example 8080.
Create a Target Group
AWS Console → EC2 → Target Groups
Click Create target group
Target type: Instances
Protocol: HTTP
Port: 8080 (if your application uses 8080)
Health check path: / or /health
Register your EC2
In the target group, choose Register targets
Select your EC2 instance
Select port 8080
Click Include as pending
Click Register pending targets
Create the Load Balancer
EC2 → Load Balancers
Create Application Load Balancer
Select your VPC
Select at least 2 Availability Zones/subnets
Select the Load Balancer Security Group.

Connect Load Balancer to Target Group
When creating the ALB:

Listener: HTTP : 80
Forward to your target group.

So:

Client → ALB :80 → Target Group :8080 → EC2 :8080

Security Groups

Load Balancer SG:

Inbound
HTTP 80 → 0.0.0.0/0

EC2 SG:

Inbound
TCP 8080 → Source: Load Balancer Security Group

This is important: don't allow port 8080 from everyone if only the load balancer needs to access your application.

