What is a Load Balancer?

A Load Balancer receives requests from users and forwards those requests to available backend servers (such as EC2 instances).

                Users
                  |
                  v
          +----------------+
          | Load Balancer  |
          +----------------+
             /     |     \
            /      |      \
           v       v       v
        EC2-1    EC2-2    EC2-3

The user normally sends the request to the Load Balancer, not directly to a particular EC2 instance.

2. Why do we need a Load Balancer?

Suppose you have only one EC2:

100 users
    |
    v
  EC2

If 1,000 or 10,000 users come at the same time, that one EC2 may become overloaded.

So we create multiple EC2 instances:

              Load Balancer
             /      |      \
            v       v       v
          EC2-1   EC2-2   EC2-3

Now the traffic can be spread across multiple servers.

Main reasons:

Handle more traffic
Prevent one server from becoming overloaded
Improve availability
Automatically avoid unhealthy servers
Allow scaling by adding/removing EC2 instances
3. How does it distribute traffic?

Suppose three EC2 instances are available:

EC2-1
EC2-2
EC2-3

Users send requests:

Request 1 ──> Load Balancer ──> EC2-1
Request 2 ──> Load Balancer ──> EC2-2
Request 3 ──> Load Balancer ──> EC2-3
Request 4 ──> Load Balancer ──> EC2-1
Request 5 ──> Load Balancer ──> EC2-2

The exact algorithm depends on the type/configuration of load balancer and routing rules.
For an AWS Application Load Balancer, it uses the configured target group and distributes requests among healthy targets.




