63. What is Auto Scaling?

Auto Scaling is an AWS feature that automatically increases or decreases the number of EC2 instances based on demand.

Example:

Normal traffic → 2 EC2 instances
Traffic increases → Auto Scaling launches more instances, e.g. 4
Traffic decreases → Auto Scaling terminates unnecessary instances, e.g. back to 2

Interview answer:

Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of EC2 instances according to application demand, helping maintain performance and control cost.

64. What is an Auto Scaling Group?

An Auto Scaling Group (ASG) is a group that manages EC2 instances automatically.

It defines things like:

Minimum capacity → minimum number of instances
Desired capacity → normal number of instances
Maximum capacity → maximum number of instances

Example:

Minimum = 2
Desired = 2
Maximum = 5

If traffic increases:

2 EC2 → 3 EC2 → 4 EC2 → 5 EC2

If traffic decreases:

5 EC2 → 4 EC2 → 3 EC2 → 2 EC2

The ASG can also replace an EC2 instance if it becomes unhealthy.

65. What is Scaling Out?

Scaling out means adding more EC2 instances to handle increased traffic.

Before:
[EC2] [EC2]

Traffic ↑

After:
[EC2] [EC2] [EC2] [EC2]

This is also called horizontal scaling.

66. What is Scaling In?

Scaling in means removing EC2 instances when traffic decreases.

Before:
[EC2] [EC2] [EC2] [EC2]

Traffic ↓

After:
[EC2] [EC2]

This reduces unnecessary resource usage and cost.

67. Difference Between Vertical and Horizontal Scaling
Scaling	Meaning	Example
Vertical scaling	Increase/decrease the power of one server	t3.micro → t3.large
Horizontal scaling	Add/remove servers	2 EC2 → 4 EC2
Easy way to remember:

Vertical = Bigger machine

Small EC2 → Large EC2

Horizontal = More machines

1 EC2 → 2 EC2 → 3 EC2

Auto Scaling Groups mainly use horizontal scaling.

68. How does Auto Scaling improve availability?

Auto Scaling improves availability by maintaining the required number of healthy EC2 instances.

For example:

Load Balancer
      |
 ┌────┼────┐
 ↓    ↓    ↓
EC2  EC2  EC2

Suppose one EC2 instance fails:

EC2 ❌

The Auto Scaling Group can detect the unhealthy instance and launch a replacement EC2 instance.

EC2 ❌  →  New EC2 ✅

Also, you can configure the ASG across multiple Availability Zones, so if one AZ has a problem, instances in another AZ can continue serving traffic.

Interview answer:

Auto Scaling improves availability by maintaining the desired number of healthy instances, replacing unhealthy instances automatically, and distributing instances across multiple Availability Zones.






8. Database
69. What is RDS?

Amazon RDS (Relational Database Service) is an AWS managed service for running relational databases.

It supports databases such as:

MySQL
PostgreSQL
MariaDB
Oracle
SQL Server
Aurora

AWS manages many administrative tasks such as backups, patching, and infrastructure maintenance.

Interview answer:

Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service in AWS that makes it easier to set up, operate, and scale relational databases.

70. Difference between RDS and DynamoDB?
RDS	DynamoDB
Relational database	NoSQL database
Uses tables with rows and columns	Uses tables with items and attributes
Supports SQL	Does not use traditional SQL as its primary query model
Supports relationships between data	Designed for key-value/document data
Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL	AWS DynamoDB
Good for structured relational applications	Good for highly scalable applications with flexible data
Simple example

RDS:

Customers
-------------------------
ID | Name | Email
1  | Siva | siva@gmail.com
2  | Ravi | ravi@gmail.com

DynamoDB:

Customer
{
  "id": 1,
  "name": "Siva",
  "email": "siva@gmail.com"
}

Easy memory:

RDS = SQL / relational
DynamoDB = NoSQL / highly scalable

71. What is a Multi-AZ deployment?

Multi-AZ means having a database deployment across multiple Availability Zones for high availability.

Example:

             RDS
              |
       ┌──────┴──────┐
       ↓             ↓
   AZ-1           AZ-2
 Primary         Standby
    DB              DB

If the primary database fails, RDS can fail over to the standby.

The standby is mainly for high availability, not for handling normal read traffic.
Primary = currently active database
Standby = ready to take over if Primary fails
Interview answer:

Multi-AZ provides high availability by maintaining a standby database in another Availability Zone and automatically failing over when the primary database has a problem.

72. What is a Read Replica?

A Read Replica is a copy of a database that is used mainly to handle read requests.

Example:

              Application
             /           \
          Write          Read
            ↓              ↓
        Primary DB     Read Replica

Suppose your application receives many SELECT queries.

Instead of sending all reads to the primary database, you can send some reads to the Read Replica.

This helps improve read performance and reduce load on the primary database.

73. Difference between Multi-AZ and Read Replica?

This is an important interview question.

Multi-AZ	Read Replica
Mainly for high availability	Mainly for read performance/scaling
Has a standby database	Has a readable replica
Used for failover	Used to handle read traffic
Standby generally doesn't serve normal read traffic	Replica can serve read requests
Automatic failover	Not primarily a failover mechanism
Easy memory:
Multi-AZ      → Availability
Read Replica  → Read performance

Example:

Multi-AZ:

Primary DB ─────→ Standby DB
     ↓
   Failure
     ↓
Standby becomes primary
Read Replica:

             ┌→ Read Replica 1
Primary DB ──┼→ Read Replica 2
             └→ Application reads
74. What is Amazon Aurora?

Amazon Aurora is an AWS-managed relational database engine designed to be compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL.

It is part of Amazon RDS.

Amazon RDS
   |
   ├── MySQL
   ├── PostgreSQL
   ├── Oracle
   ├── SQL Server
   ├── MariaDB
   └── Aurora
          ├── Aurora MySQL
          └── Aurora PostgreSQL

Aurora is designed for high performance, availability, and scalability.

Interview answer:

Amazon Aurora is a fully managed relational database engine from AWS that is compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL and is designed for high performance and high availability.

What is a Read Replica?

A Read Replica is a copy of your primary database that is mainly used to handle READ requests.

Suppose your application has one database:

             Application
                  |
                  ↓
             Primary DB
                  |
        ┌─────────┴─────────┐
        ↓                   ↓
      READ                WRITE
   SELECT query       INSERT/UPDATE

If thousands of users are reading data, the Primary DB has to handle all those requests.

A Read Replica helps by taking some of the read traffic away from the Primary.

Why do we need it?

Imagine your application gets:

10,000 READ requests
   +
1,000 WRITE requests

If everything goes to one database:

              Application
                   |
                   ↓
              Primary DB
                   ↑
          11,000 requests

The Primary DB has a lot of work.

Instead, we can use Read Replicas:

                 Application
                /     |      \
               ↓      ↓       ↓
             Reads   Reads   Writes
               ↓      ↓       ↓
            Replica  Replica  Primary

Now the Read Replicas handle many of the read requests.

Common READ operations
SELECT → READ
View product details → READ
View profile → READ
View order history → READ

It takes a copy of the database. It does not use the same database storage.

Read Replica
             Primary Database
             (original data)
                    |
                    | copies/replicates data
                    ↓
             Read Replica
             (copy of data)

The Read Replica is a separate database instance containing a copy of the primary database's data.

Then the application can do:

READ request
     ↓
Read Replica
     ↓
Gets the data

You create a Read Replica from the RDS console, using your existing primary database.

Steps in AWS
Go to AWS Console
Search RDS
Select Databases
Click your existing DB instance
Click Actions
Choose Create read replica
Select the settings for the replica
Click Create read replica

If you create RDS MySQL, you're using:

AWS RDS service
       ↓
MySQL database engine

If you create Aurora, you're using:

AWS RDS service
       ↓
Aurora database engine
       ↓
AWS's own technology

So RDS itself is AWS, but some of the database engines that RDS supports were developed by other organizations.



What is DynamoDB?

Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database service in AWS.

It is used by applications to store and retrieve data quickly at large scale.

Simple example

Suppose your application needs to store users:

DynamoDB
   ↓
Users Table
   ↓
Item
{
   UserID: 101,
   Name: "Siva",
   Email: "siva@gmail.com"
}

Here:

Table → Users
Item → one user's data
Attributes → UserID, Name, Email
Partition key → uniquely identifies/finds the item, such as UserID
Why do we use DynamoDB?

For applications that need:

Very fast data access
Large numbers of users/requests
Automatic scaling
High availability
No database server management
DynamoDB vs RDS
RDS
 ↓
Relational / SQL
 ↓
Tables + Rows + Columns
 ↓
MySQL, PostgreSQL, etc.




DynamoDB
 ↓
NoSQL
 ↓
Tables + Items + Attributes
 ↓
Fast key-based access

Interview answer:

DynamoDB is a fully managed, serverless NoSQL database service provided by AWS. It provides fast and scalable data storage and retrieval without requiring us to manage database servers.

